{"hash":"b8cd67ad1db493b4","voice":"en-IN-NeerjaExpressiveNeural","rate":"+4%","dur":87.047,"said":"You are inside the assembly hall. Along the walls stand images of the five brothers we just met, with Draupadi, their queen, and Krishna, who stood beside them through the war. Their story is kept right here, next to the Lord they sought. Now think of the year this temple lives. For half of it, snow buries everything you have seen. So each autumn, on Bhai Dooj, two days after Diwali, the doors are closed with Vedic chants. The Lord’s five-faced festival image is seated in a palanquin, the Panchmukhi Doli, and carried down the mountain on devotees’ shoulders, halting on the way, to the Omkareshwar temple at Ukhimath. There Baba Kedar is worshipped all winter. On Mahashivratri, the priests at Ukhimath announce the day the doors will open again, and in spring, around late April or May, the doli climbs back up. Through the open season the day begins before dawn: from four in the morning the Lord is bathed in the abhishek pujas, the Mahabhishek and the Rudrabhishek; later he is offered his meal, the bhog; and in the evening he is adorned and the aarti is sung. Now walk forward, toward the narrow door. The rock of the legend waits inside.\n\nDid you know? At Ukhimath, Baba Kedar spends the winter together with Madhyamaheshwar, the second Kedar.","segments":[{"key":"text","start":0,"end":1150},{"key":"didYouKnow","start":1166,"end":1256}],"words":[[0.098,0.18,0,3],[0.29,0.108,4,7],[0.411,0.469,8,14],[0.891,0.096,15,18],[1.0,0.505,19,27],[1.516,0.409,28,32],[2.837,0.373,34,39],[3.221,0.12,40,43],[3.353,0.312,44,49],[3.678,0.433,50,55],[4.123,0.553,56,62],[4.688,0.096,63,65],[4.796,0.096,66,69],[4.904,0.337,70,74],[5.252,0.457,75,83],[5.721,0.132,84,86],[5.865,0.361,87,91],[6.238,0.325,92,95],[6.803,0.12,97,101],[6.935,0.565,102,110],[7.752,0.156,112,117],[7.921,0.505,118,123],[8.654,0.228,125,128],[8.894,0.493,129,136],[9.579,0.096,138,141],[9.688,0.288,142,147],[9.988,0.445,